---
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
import { constraints } from "../../lib/model";
import { measurement, money } from "../../lib/cases";
import { siteConfig } from "../../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title="The programme and the practice"
  description="The fictional loan programme your clients are applying to, the fictional consultancy you work for, and the rules they set."
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <p class="fiction">
    <strong>Teaching fiction.</strong> The Household Resilience Loan Programme and Common Ground
    Engineering do not exist. They are a frame for the engineering: they give every design a client,
    a deadline, a budget and a reviewer. No real government policy, lender or endorsement is implied,
    and nothing in this course is financial advice.
  </p>

  <h2>The Household Resilience Loan Programme</h2>
  <p>
    A government programme in this course's fiction lends to homeowners for retrofits that make a
    household more self-reliant in food, water and waste. Loans are selective. An application stands
    or falls on an engineering proposal showing that the retrofit meets the household's own service
    needs, costs what it says, and will still be working in five years.
  </p>
  <p>The programme's rules are the course's hard constraints:</p>
  <Figure caption="Programme rules. They apply to every home and every alternative." basis="course assumption">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Rule</th><th scope="col">Limit</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Implementation cost</th><td>at most {money(constraints.budget_aud)}, including a {(constraints.contingency * 100).toFixed(0)} % contingency, priced from the <a href={withBase("/method/costing/")}>course schedule</a></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Process energy</th><td>at most {constraints.energy_cap_kwh_day} kWh a day, averaged over the peak month, counting the loads that run the food, water and waste systems</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Preliminary investigation</th><td>a separate {money(measurement.allowance)} per home for measurement equipment and services; consultant time is scheduled in hours, not charged</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Scope</th><td>food, water and waste only; energy and shelter belong to a later round of the programme</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Figure>
  <p>
    Borrowing more is allowed up to the limit, but within this scheme a larger loan is a larger
    exposure, so the programme asks a stronger question of it: what does the extra money buy that
    a cheaper option does not? That is an engineering question about alternatives. It is not a
    lesson in how real credit is assessed, and the course has no interest rates, credit scores or
    income tests.
  </p>
  <p>
    What an application gets from you is the <strong>technical annex and executive
    recommendation</strong>. It is not a loan approval, and it is not a construction-certified
    design: pipe sizes, wiring, structural detail and certification belong to licensed trades later.
    You specify performance and how to check it has been achieved.
  </p>

  <h2>Common Ground Engineering</h2>
  <p>
    You are a graduate engineer at Common Ground, a small consultancy that prepares programme
    applications. Each graduate takes one household from intake to proposal under a senior reviewer,
    played by the teaching team, who reads your drafts the way a programme assessor will.
  </p>
  <p>The work runs in two stages, a year apart in the story and seven weeks apart in the course:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Measure first.</strong> From the intake dossier you design a year-long measurement
      programme (<a href={withBase("/assessments/")}>Assignment 1</a>, due week 7):
      what to measure, where, how often, how well, and what it costs.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Twelve months later.</strong> The story jumps a year. Common Ground's specialist team
      hands over its analysed findings for your home (Release B), and you design the retrofit and
      make the case for it (<a href={withBase("/assessments/")}>Assignment 2</a>).
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    You do not wait a year or collect real data. The specialists' measured year is synthetic,
    generated to be consistent with each home, and labelled that way everywhere it appears.
  </p>

  <h2>The people in the story</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Your client</strong>, one of <a href={withBase("/clients/")}>five households</a>, each with priorities that are theirs, not the course's.</li>
    <li><strong>The senior reviewer</strong>, who asks the questions a programme assessor would, in design reviews in weeks 7 and 12.</li>
    <li><strong>The specialist team</strong>, who run the measurement year and analyse it. They report conditions and implications. They do not choose the system.</li>
  </ul>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .fiction {
    padding: 0.8rem 1rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--at-primary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
  }

  th[scope="row"] {
    text-align: left;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>
